WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.759
Welcome to Class 14: efficient I/O—copy locally, compute locally, return results.

2
00:00:05.759 --> 00:00:10.470
This video introduces the core decisions and working patterns.

3
00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:17.799
Watch the complete lesson first, then use the written class page for copyable commands,

4
00:00:17.799 --> 00:00:20.417
exercises, and detailed reference material.

5
00:00:20.417 --> 00:00:27.628
After this class, you should be able to: distinguish durable shared storage from temporary node-local scratch;

6
00:00:27.628 --> 00:00:34.840
explain streaming, random, small-file, and metadata I/O; recognize when storage rather than CPU or GPU limits

7
00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:40.699
a job; write an sbatch job that stages inputs and returns outputs safely;

8
00:00:40.699 --> 00:00:47.010
use rsync, checksums, shell traps, and atomic publication; apply the same pattern in Snakemake;

9
00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:54.221
and explain how poor I/O structure can turn hours of computation into weeks or months of

10
00:00:54.221 --> 00:00:55.123
elapsed time.

11
00:00:55.123 --> 00:00:57.986
A job does not merely execute instructions.

12
00:00:57.986 --> 00:01:04.123
It repeatedly moves data among: shared project storage; the network; the compute node's page cache;

13
00:01:04.123 --> 00:01:07.395
local disk; RAM; and CPU or GPU processes.

14
00:01:07.395 --> 00:01:11.895
The slowest required part of that path limits the complete job.

15
00:01:11.895 --> 00:01:18.441
Adding CPU cores does not help when those cores spend most of their time waiting for

16
00:01:18.441 --> 00:01:18.850
files.

17
00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:23.759
Shared storage is designed to make durable data available to many nodes.

18
00:01:23.759 --> 00:01:29.895
It is not designed to behave like a private local SSD for every job simultaneously.

19
00:01:29.895 --> 00:01:34.395
One badly structured job can also create contention for other researchers.

20
00:01:34.395 --> 00:01:41.062
### Streaming I/O Streaming I/O reads or writes a long sequence of bytes in order:

21
00:01:41.062 --> 00:01:46.395
Examples include: reading a compressed FASTQ file once from beginning to end;

22
00:01:46.395 --> 00:01:53.061
writing one BAM, CRAM, archive, or report; piping decompressed data directly into the next program;

23
00:01:53.061 --> 00:01:55.283
and copying one large file.

24
00:01:55.283 --> 00:02:02.394
Streaming works well because storage devices, operating-system readahead, compression libraries, and network protocols can transfer large

25
00:02:02.394 --> 00:02:03.283
blocks efficiently.

26
00:02:03.283 --> 00:02:10.394
The cost of opening the file is paid once and useful throughput can approach the storage

27
00:02:10.394 --> 00:02:11.283
system's bandwidth.

28
00:02:11.283 --> 00:02:16.616
### Random I/O Random I/O repeatedly jumps among unrelated offsets or files:

29
00:02:16.616 --> 00:02:21.061
Examples include: repeatedly querying tiny genomic regions across a large.

30
00:02:21.061 --> 00:02:28.979
Suppose one sample requires: 2 seconds of useful CPU work; and 20 seconds waiting for small

31
00:02:28.979 --> 00:02:29.969
network-storage operations.

32
00:02:29.969 --> 00:02:35.908
For 5,000 samples and 10 workflow stages: That estimate excludes queueing, retries,

33
00:02:35.908 --> 00:02:40.857
directory scans, environment startup, and contention caused by parallel jobs.

34
00:02:40.857 --> 00:02:47.291
If several rules repeat the same anti-pattern or failures force stages to restart,

35
00:02:47.291 --> 00:02:54.219
a workflow expected to finish in hours can remain active for weeks or months.

36
00:02:54.219 --> 00:03:00.158
Typical multipliers are: number of samples; files per sample; workflow stages; retries;

37
00:03:00.158 --> 00:03:03.623
parallel workers; and repeated decompression or copying.

38
00:03:03.623 --> 00:03:07.087
Measure one representative sample before launching thousands.

39
00:03:07.087 --> 00:03:11.023
The following job uses the scratch directory supplied by Slurm.

40
00:03:11.023 --> 00:03:17.322
The fallback matches the current RCC job-local layout and is used only when SLURM_TMPDIR is not

41
00:03:17.322 --> 00:03:17.715
set.

42
00:03:17.715 --> 00:03:24.014
Submit it from a login or submission host: The compute must occur inside the Slurm job,

43
00:03:24.014 --> 00:03:27.163
not directly on the login or submission host.

44
00:03:27.163 --> 00:03:33.412
The explicit staging pattern is easy to audit: Where available and tested on RCC,

45
00:03:33.412 --> 00:03:39.216
Snakemake shadow rules and the tmpdir resource can provide a more declarative implementation.

46
00:03:39.216 --> 00:03:45.019
The same invariants remain: active temporary work is local; durable inputs remain unchanged;

47
00:03:45.019 --> 00:03:52.161
only required outputs return to shared storage; and failed jobs do not publish apparently complete results.

48
00:03:52.161 --> 00:03:59.359
Repeatedly transferring or rebuilding the same immutable data wastes both network capacity and wall-clock time.

49
00:03:59.359 --> 00:04:05.597
Suitable reusable objects include: Apptainer images; read-only reference genomes; annotation databases; aligner indexes;

50
00:04:05.597 --> 00:04:13.274
model weights; software archives; and decompressed copies of compressed references when repeated local access justifies the

51
00:04:13.274 --> 00:04:13.754
space.

52
00:04:13.754 --> 00:04:17.593
A cache is not a substitute for provenance.

53
00:04:17.593 --> 00:04:23.351
Every cached object must be tied to an immutable identity such as:

54
00:04:23.351 --> 00:04:29.109
a cryptographic checksum; a container digest; an exact version; a versioned path;

55
00:04:29.109 --> 00:04:31.508
or a workflow-managed content hash.

56
00:04:31.508 --> 00:04:37.746
### Node-local cache pattern Use a stable cache directory separate from job-specific scratch:

57
00:04:37.746 --> 00:04:42.544
The temporary file plus atomic rename prevents concurrent jobs from.

58
00:04:42.544 --> 00:04:48.236
Start with Slurm accounting: Inside an active allocation, useful tools may include:

59
00:04:48.236 --> 00:04:53.927
Interpretation: Use representative pilot data and change one variable at a time.

60
00:04:53.927 --> 00:05:00.458
Use local scratch when: the tool creates many temporary files; the tool performs repeated random reads

61
00:05:00.458 --> 00:05:05.355
or writes; the input will be reread many times during one job;

62
00:05:05.355 --> 00:05:11.886
a temporary database or index is built per job; or local staging measurably reduces elapsed time

63
00:05:11.886 --> 00:05:13.110
and shared-storage pressure.

64
00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:19.641
Keep direct shared-storage streaming when: the tool performs one sequential pass; input is much larger than

65
00:05:19.641 --> 00:05:26.171
local capacity; staging would duplicate most of the job's total I/O; several nodes must concurrently read

66
00:05:26.171 --> 00:05:32.293
one durable source; or RCC policy identifies the storage tier as suitable for that pattern.

